From: Alex Scotti <alexscotti@gmail.com>
Subject: amdv: keep the EL of a stream the player calls FEL

The Dolby Vision core tells the base-layer decoder to stop feeding the
enhancement layer whenever its metadata parser returns MEL for an RPU
("bypass MEL"). For a real minimum enhancement layer that is right. It is not a
cheap hint, though: the receiving decoder latches bypass_dvenl_enable, which
drops it from DECODE_MODE_MULTI_DVBAL to plain streambase, and a streambase base
layer never reports HEVC_FIND_NEXT_DVEL_NAL - the only event that ever hands the
shared core to the enhancement decoder. The layer pair stops alternating
permanently and nothing clears the latch.

On a profile 7 FEL Blu-ray the verdict is wrong. Measured on John Wick 3 on an
AM9 Pro (S6), which the player opens as "profile 7, full enhancement layer":

    honoured:  dveldec ready 118462  scheduled 5     20.65fps, 165 stutters/65s
    ignored:   dveldec ready  40135  scheduled 1469  23.99fps, 0 stutters

Three events are enough to latch it and the first arrives inside the first
second of the feature, so the whole film plays base-layer-only with a repeated
frame roughly every 400ms.

The closed parser cannot be second-guessed from in here, and does not need to be:
the player already tells us. enable_fel is set for exactly those sessions whose
RPU it read as FEL, and cleared when the decoder closes. This file already
consults it four times to stop a FEL stream being dropped to SDR, so the same
knowledge belongs in this decision - a MEL stream keeps the optimisation it is
entitled to, and a FEL stream keeps its enhancement layer.

Both call sites here are patched. amdv_hw5_process.c carries the same two blocks
but cannot see enable_fel (static to this file); the hardware this was measured
on takes the paths here, logging "bypass MEL", which is this file.
---
--- a/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c
+++ b/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c
@@ -8284,7 +8284,26 @@
 			if (force_mel)
 				ret_flags = 1;
 
-			if (ret_flags && req.dv_enhance_exist) {
+			/*
+			 * Not when the player has declared this session FEL.
+			 * ret_flags is the Dolby metadata parser verdict and 1
+			 * means "minimum enhancement layer": no residual, so
+			 * the base layer may as well run alone. Acting on it is
+			 * not free - the receiving decoder latches
+			 * bypass_dvenl_enable, which drops it out of
+			 * DECODE_MODE_MULTI_DVBAL, and a streambase base layer
+			 * never reports HEVC_FIND_NEXT_DVEL_NAL, the only event
+			 * that hands the shared core to the enhancement
+			 * decoder. The pair then stops alternating for good.
+			 *
+			 * On a profile 7 FEL disc that verdict is wrong, and
+			 * enable_fel is how we know: the player sets it for
+			 * exactly the streams whose RPU it read as FEL, and
+			 * clears it when the decoder closes. It already guards
+			 * the FEL paths just below that would otherwise fall
+			 * back to SDR; the same knowledge belongs here.
+			 */
+			if (ret_flags && req.dv_enhance_exist && !enable_fel) {
 				if (!strcmp(dv_provider[0], "dvbldec"))
 					vf_notify_provider_by_name
 					(dv_provider[0],
@@ -9935,7 +9954,26 @@
